     "rate": "+0%",
     "text": "Three thousand years ago, a merchant in Babylon bought a field using a raw lump of silver. Most people think money started as paper bills, but early trade relied on cattle and barley. Herding cows to pay debts got exhausting, so merchants started weighing raw silver metal. One shekel equaled exactly one hundred and eighty grains of barley, setting a fixed value. Then around six hundred BC, Lydia minted the first official coins to stop silver fraud. Centuries later, Chinese traders in the Tang dynasty created paper notes because metal got too heavy. Your modern cash is just a paper shadow of ancient silver."
